Good plan — I can help you prepare a concise checklist and the right questions to bring to Dr. Amy Foster. Before I make anything specific, a few quick clarifying questions (these will change recommendations):

- Are you currently taking an anticoagulant for atrial fibrillation? If so, which drug and dose?
- What are your home or clinic blood pressure readings (typical values)?
- What antihypertensive medicines are you already taking (drug names and doses)?
- Do you know whether your heart failure is reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF) or preserved (HFpEF)?
- Can you confirm the units and date of the creatinine of 110 (e.g., µmol/L vs mg/dL) and any recent eGFR?
- Current hemoglobin/Hct and any iron studies (ferritin, iron, TIBC)? When was the anemia first noted?
- Any GI symptoms: black or bloody stools, melena, change in bowel habits, abdominal pain, weight loss?
- When was your last colonoscopy (if ever), and any family history of colon cancer or GI disease?
- Any regular NSAID use or recent GI bleeding history?

Below are focused questions and talking points you can use for your annual physical. I grouped them into hypertension and GI/anemia so they’re easy to bring up.

Hypertension management — questions to ask Dr. Foster
- What BP target do you recommend for me, given my age (60), atrial fibrillation, and history of heart failure? (If helpful: recent guidelines generally aim for <130/80 when tolerated, but targets are individualized for heart failure.)
- Based on my current BP readings and symptoms, do you recommend changing or starting any antihypertensive medications?
- Which classes of blood‑pressure meds do you prefer for someone with AF and heart failure? (e.g., ACE inhibitor/ARB/ARNI, beta‑blocker, diuretic) — and why for my situation.
- Are there any antihypertensives to avoid given my heart failure type (HFrEF vs HFpEF), my kidney function, or my other meds?
- I take metformin and am on a prednisone taper — do these affect BP control or choice of antihypertensive? (Prednisone can raise BP and cause fluid retention.)
- How should we monitor my kidney function and electrolytes if we change BP meds (timing and frequency)?
- Is a beta‑blocker helpful for both rate control of AF and BP? If I’m already on a rate control medication from cardiology, how should primary care coordinate changes?
- Are there specific symptoms that should prompt urgent BP medication changes (dizziness, lightheadedness, worsening shortness of breath from fluid retention)?
- Would you recommend home BP monitoring? If yes, what schedule and how should I record readings to share with you?
- Do you want me to loop in my cardiologist when we make any BP medication changes?

GI evaluation / Referral to GI — questions to ask Dr. Foster
- Given my age (60) and anemia, do you recommend a GI evaluation (e.g., colonoscopy and/or upper endoscopy)? How urgent is this?
- Are my anemia labs consistent with iron‑deficiency anemia (ask for ferritin/iron/TIBC) — if so, would you refer me to GI for endoscopic evaluation to look for an occult bleed?
- If I’m taking or will take an anticoagulant for AF, how will that be managed for endoscopy? Should we coordinate with cardiology prior to scheduling procedures?
- Do you want stool occult blood testing or other initial tests before referral (fecal immunochemical test, stool guaiac)?
- If I haven’t had a colonoscopy recently (or ever), do you recommend scheduling one now for screening regardless of anemia?
- What are the options if colonoscopy isn’t feasible (CT colonography)? Would GI evaluate the upper GI tract as well (EGD) given steroid use or upper GI bleed risk?
- If you do refer me to Boston GI, do you have a preferred specialist there, and can you provide a short referral note summarizing my anemia, meds, anticoagulation status, and recent labs?
- What should I do in the meantime to manage anemia symptoms (e.g., iron supplementation) and are there any immediate red flags to watch for (significant dark/black stools, bright red bleeding, worsening fatigue, syncope)?

Practical items to bring to the appointment
- A printout/list of current medications (including dose and over‑the‑counter meds and supplements like iron or NSAIDs)
- Recent home BP logs and any clinic BP readings
- Recent labs: CBC (Hgb/Hct), ferritin, iron/TIBC, creatinine, eGFR, electrolytes, A1c
- Date of last colonoscopy and results (if any)
- Name of your cardiologist and recent cardiology notes (if available)
- A list of symptoms (GI, lightheadedness, weight changes, edema, worsening shortness of breath)

Brief rationale / what to expect
- Age 60 + new or unexplained anemia should prompt GI evaluation for occult bleeding (colonoscopy is the usual first step for lower GI evaluation). If iron‑deficiency is confirmed, GI referral is commonly recommended.
- Prednisone can raise blood pressure and cause fluid retention, complicating hypertension and heart failure control; it can also affect glucose control (relevant if on metformin).
- Kidney function (creatinine/eGFR) affects choice and dosing of some antihypertensives (e.g., ACEi/ARB, diuretics) and affects metformin safety thresholds. Confirming units/values enables safer decisions.
- If you’re on anticoagulation, GI procedures require coordination to balance bleeding and clotting risk — that’s a good reason to have PCP and cardiology discuss referrals.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short email or message to Dr. Amy Foster summarizing these concerns and asking for a GI referral and guidance on BP management? (If yes, tell me whether you want it sent now and whether to include any labs or exact BP readings.)
- Or make a one‑page checklist you can print and bring to the visit?

Tell me which of the clarifying details above you can share, and whether you want me to draft the email or checklist.

