Good plan — I’ll walk through the key issues and give you a short checklist and suggested questions to bring to Dr. Foster, plus what to ask/expect if you’re referred to Boston GI.

Quick summary of the clinical context you gave
- Age 60, atrial fibrillation, history of heart failure, on metformin 500 mg twice daily, currently on a prednisone taper for anemia. Last creatinine 110 (please tell me units — μmol/L vs mg/dL — and whether you have an eGFR available).  
- Important unknowns that change recommendations: whether you are on an anticoagulant (warfarin or a DOAC), current blood pressure readings and current antihypertensive meds, type of anemia (iron deficiency vs other), hemoglobin and iron studies, and any GI symptoms (black stools, visible blood, change in bowel habits, weight loss).

Hypertension — items to review and discuss with Dr. Foster
- BP target: given your AF and heart failure, typical targets are ≈ <130/80 if tolerated, but individualized. Ask whether the team wants a tighter target or a more conservative one because of heart failure symptoms or orthostatic issues.
- Current med review: ask Dr. Foster to review all current meds (including over-the-counter meds, prednisone dose and duration). Important points:
  - ACE inhibitors or ARBs are often preferred for patients with diabetes and heart failure (they lower BP and provide cardiac/renal benefit) but require checking creatinine and potassium after starting or dose changes.
  - Beta-blockers can help both heart failure and rate control for AF (useful if you need rate control).
  - Diuretics (loop or thiazide) may be needed for volume control in heart failure — but watch kidney function and electrolytes.
  - Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists (spironolactone/eplerenone) have benefits in certain HF patients but require close monitoring of potassium and creatinine.
- Kidney function and metformin: metformin dosing/continuation depends on eGFR. If creatinine is 110 μmol/L (≈1.24 mg/dL) your eGFR may still be OK, but ask for eGFR and whether metformin should be continued or dose-adjusted. If eGFR <45 mL/min/1.73 m2, metformin reassessment is often needed.
- Prednisone effects: steroid taper can raise blood pressure and blood glucose — ask whether prednisone is expected to change BP and if monitoring should be intensified during/after the taper.
- Monitoring plan: ask for a home BP plan — target readings, frequency (e.g., twice daily for 1–2 weeks), how to record them, and whether ambulatory BP monitoring or orthostatic BP checks are needed.
- Labs and safety checks before/after medication changes:
  - Basic metabolic panel (creatinine, eGFR, potassium)
  - A1c if diabetes management is relevant
  - Liver tests as indicated
  - BNP or NT-proBNP and/or echocardiogram if heart failure status is unclear or worsened
- Interactions / cautions:
  - Avoid NSAIDs if possible (can raise BP and worsen heart failure and renal function).
  - If you are on an anticoagulant (likely with AF), coordinate any medication changes or procedures with the prescribing clinician.

Suggested direct questions to bring to Dr. Foster about hypertension
- “What BP target do you recommend for me given my atrial fibrillation, heart failure, diabetes and current prednisone taper?”
- “Do you want me to start or change any antihypertensive medications now? If yes, what are the expected side effects and what labs do you want me to get and when?”
- “Please check my most recent eGFR and potassium — is metformin still okay at my current kidney function?”
- “Could prednisone be causing my blood pressure to rise, and should we wait until the taper is finished before making long-term BP changes?”
- “Do you want me to record home BPs? If so, how often and what threshold should prompt a phone call?”
- “Should I be co-managed with cardiology or nephrology for tighter BP control given my heart failure and creatinine?”

GI referral — do you need one now?
Why a GI referral is commonly recommended in people like you
- Age 60: you are within the range for routine colorectal cancer screening (typically colonoscopy up to age 75).  
- Anemia: unexplained iron-deficiency anemia or any new anemia in someone age ≥50 commonly prompts GI evaluation to look for GI blood loss (upper and lower GI sources). Prednisone would not explain iron-deficiency anemia; it may mask symptoms.  
- Anticoagulation (if present) can increase risk of GI bleeding and complicate colonoscopy planning — coordination is needed.

When referral is more urgent vs routine
- Urgent referral more likely if:
  - Your anemia is iron-deficiency (low ferritin, low iron), hemoglobin is significantly low or falling, or you have GI symptoms (melena, hematochezia, black/tarry stools, unexplained weight loss, persistent change in bowel habits).
  - Positive fecal occult blood test.
- Routine referral appropriate if:
  - No overt GI bleeding, anemia is mild and workup is ongoing, and you need average-priority colon cancer screening because of age.

What GI will likely recommend / do
- Tests typically ordered:
  - CBC and iron studies (ferritin, iron, TIBC) if not already done.
  - Colonoscopy (gold standard for colorectal evaluation).
  - Possibly upper endoscopy (EGD) if upper GI bleeding is suspected or if colonoscopy is normal and iron deficiency persists.
  - Fecal immunochemical test (FIT) might be used if immediate colonoscopy scheduling is an issue, but FIT is not adequate if iron-deficiency anemia is present — colonoscopy is preferred.
- Coordination with anticoagulation: GI will need clear instructions about when/how to hold anticoagulant and whether bridging is required — this requires coordination among GI, cardiology/PCP.
- Pre-procedure needs: medication list, prior colonoscopy reports, anesthesia clearance if needed, kidney function labs if contrast is planned for any imaging.

Suggested questions to ask when requesting a Boston GI referral
- To your PCP when asking for the referral:
  - “Given my age and unexplained anemia, can you refer me to Boston GI for colonoscopy and evaluation of possible GI blood loss?”
  - “Is this urgent or can it be scheduled routinely? My hemoglobin is __ and ferritin is __ (fill in if known).”
  - “I am (or am not) on anticoagulation — how should we manage this before GI procedures? Please coordinate with cardiology if needed.”
  - “Please include recent labs (CBC, iron studies, creatinine/eGFR) and note my history of AF and heart failure on the referral.”
- Questions to ask Boston GI (once referred or at visit):
  - “Do I need colonoscopy and/or EGD to evaluate my anemia?”
  - “How soon can this be scheduled given my age and labs?”
  - “What is the plan for managing my anticoagulation for the procedure?”
  - “Are there specific labs or cardiology clearances you want before the procedure?”

What to gather and bring to the appointment or include in the referral
- Current medication list (prescription, OTC, supplements — especially aspirin, NSAIDs, iron supplements, anticoagulants).
- Recent labs: CBC (hemoglobin), ferritin, iron studies, creatinine and eGFR, electrolytes, A1c if available.
- Recent BP readings (home log) and any symptoms (dizziness, lightheadedness, swelling, chest pain, shortness of breath).
- Last colonoscopy report (if you've had one) and any family history of colon cancer or polyps.

Safety and coordination notes
- If you are on an anticoagulant: do not stop it on your own. Anticoagulation management around endoscopy/colonoscopy requires a plan from your PCP/cardiologist/GI together. Some procedures need stopping the drug; some can proceed with bridging.
- If your creatinine/eGFR is impaired, some medications for BP and some procedural choices may be affected — get an up-to-date eGFR and BMP before medication changes or procedures.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a concise message or checklist you can bring to Dr. Foster covering the hypertension questions and requesting a GI referral? OR
- Help you draft the referral request language to send or bring to your PCP (including your current meds and lab numbers)?
Tell me which and provide any missing details you have (are you on anticoagulation? current BP readings? hemoglobin/ferritin values? creatinine units and eGFR).

