  - Fecal immunochemical test (FIT) might be used if immediate colonoscopy scheduling is an issue, but FIT is not adequate if iron-deficiency anemia is present — colonoscopy is preferred.
- Coordination with anticoagulation: GI will need clear instructions about when/how to hold anticoagulant and whether bridging is required — this requires coordination among GI, cardiology/PCP.
- Pre-procedure needs: medication list, prior colonoscopy reports, anesthesia clearance if needed, kidney function labs if contrast is planned for any imaging.

Suggested questions to ask when requesting a Boston GI referral
- To your PCP when asking for the referral:
  - “Given my age and unexplained anemia, can you refer me to Boston GI for colonoscopy and evaluation of possible GI blood loss?”
  - “Is this urgent or can it be scheduled routinely? My hemoglobin is __ and ferritin is __ (fill in if known).”
  - “I am (or am not) on anticoagulation — how should we manage this before GI procedures? Please coordinate with cardiology if needed.”
  - “Please include recent labs (CBC, iron studies, creatinine/eGFR) and note my history of AF and heart failure on the referral.”
- Questions to ask Boston GI (once referred or at visit):
  - “Do I need colonoscopy and/or EGD to evaluate my anemia?”
  - “How soon can this be scheduled given my age and labs?”
  - “What is the plan for managing my anticoagulation for the procedure?”
  - “Are there specific labs or cardiology clearances you want before the procedure?”

What to gather and bring to the appointment or include in the referral
- Current medication list (prescription, OTC, supplements — especially aspirin, NSAIDs, iron supplements, anticoagulants).
- Recent labs: CBC (hemoglobin), ferritin, iron studies, creatinine and eGFR, electrolytes, A1c if available.
- Recent BP readings (home log) and any symptoms (dizziness, lightheadedness, swelling, chest pain, shortness of breath).
- Last colonoscopy report (if you've had one) and any family history of colon cancer or polyps.

Safety and coordination notes
- If you are on an anticoagulant: do not stop it on your own. Anticoagulation management around endoscopy/colonoscopy requires a plan from your PCP/cardiologist/GI together. Some procedures need stopping the drug; some can proceed with bridging.
- If your creatinine/eGFR is impaired, some medications for BP and some procedural choices may be affected — get an up-to-date eGFR and BMP before medication changes or procedures.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a concise message or checklist you can bring to Dr. Foster covering the hypertension questions and requesting a GI referral? OR
- Help you draft the referral request language to send or bring to your PCP (including your current meds and lab numbers)?
Tell me which and provide any missing details you have (are you on anticoagulation? current BP readings? hemoglobin/ferritin values? creatinine units and eGFR).

